page("how-to-sear-on-a-charcoal-grill","How to Get a Hard Sear on a Small Charcoal Grill","Crust, grill marks and flavor on the Slot Grill Compact: dry surfaces, hot steel, coal depth, when to flip, and searing on the griddle plate.",IMG["c08"],f"""
<p>A good sear is the reason most people cook over charcoal in the first place: a deep brown crust, real grill marks, and flavor you can't get from a pan on a weak stove. Small charcoal grills are actually excellent searing tools, because the food sits close to the coals and the 12-gauge carbon steel of the Slot Grill Compact holds heat. Here's how to get it right every time.</p>
<h2>What a sear actually is</h2><p>Browning happens when the surface of food gets hot and dry enough for sugars and proteins to react, well above the boiling point of water. Anything that keeps the surface wet or cool (moisture, a cold grate, weak coals, a crowded grate) delays browning, and the food overcooks inside while you wait. Every tip below is about getting the surface hot and dry, fast.</p>
<h2>Step by step</h2><ol><li><b>Dry the food.</b> Pat steaks, chops, burgers and vegetables dry with paper towels. For steak, salting 40 minutes or more ahead (or overnight in the fridge, uncovered) draws moisture out and then lets it reabsorb, leaving a drier surface. If you can't wait 40 minutes, salt right before cooking.</li><li><b>Oil the food, not just the grate.</b> A thin film of high-smoke-point oil helps contact and heat transfer.</li><li><b>Build a deep hot zone.</b> Pile coals two layers deep under the sear area in a <a href="/two-zone-fire-on-a-small-grill.html">two-zone fire</a>. Lump charcoal burns hotter for this job; briquettes work too with a fuller chimney.</li><li><b>Wait for ready coals.</b> Glowing under gray ash. Check with the hand test: about 2 seconds is high heat.</li><li><b>Preheat the grates</b> for several minutes over the coals. Hot steel gives marks and releases food.</li><li><b>Scrape and oil the grates</b> with an oiled paper towel in tongs.</li><li><b>Lay the food down and leave it.</b> It will stick at first. When the crust forms, it releases.</li><li><b>Flip once the crust is set,</b> usually a couple of minutes for a steak over hot coals. For thick cuts, flip more often after the first crust for more even cooking inside.</li><li><b>Finish on the cool side</b> if the inside needs more time. Check with an <a href="{THERMO}">instant-read thermometer</a>.</li><li><b>Rest</b> steaks and chops a few minutes before slicing.</li></ol>
<h2>Grill marks vs. full crust</h2><p>Grill marks are stripes of deep browning where the grate touches. A full crust is browning across the whole surface. On a grate, you get marks plus good browning between them from radiant heat. For an edge-to-edge crust, sear on a <a href="{GRID}">Slot Grill griddle (plancha)</a> instead: it's the same thick carbon steel as the grill and gives full contact, like a cast-iron pan over live fire. The Compact is strong enough to support cast iron, so a skillet works too.</p>
<h2>Searing on the griddle plate</h2><ul><li>Put the griddle over the deeper coals and let it heat thoroughly. A drop of water should skitter and vanish.</li><li>Add a thin layer of oil just before the food.</li><li>Press thin items (smash burgers, skirt steak) flat for full contact.</li><li>Keep the griddle seasoned so food releases; see <a href="/how-to-season-a-compact-grill.html">seasoning</a>.</li></ul>
<h2>What sears best</h2><div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>Food</th><th>Tip</th></tr><tr><td>Ribeye, strip, sirloin</td><td>Salt ahead, sear hot, finish cool side. See <a href="/reverse-sear-steak-small-grill.html">ribeye</a>.</td></tr><tr><td>Skirt and flank</td><td>Thin: all-hot, a few minutes per side. See <a href="/steak-fajitas-compact-grill.html">fajitas</a>.</td></tr><tr><td>Smash burgers</td><td>Griddle, smash hard, don't move until crusted. See <a href="/smash-burgers-on-a-griddle.html">recipe</a>.</td></tr><tr><td>Pork chops</td><td>Thick chops; sear then cool side. See <a href="/pork-chops-small-charcoal-grill.html">recipe</a>.</td></tr><tr><td>Shrimp and fish</td><td>Very dry, well-oiled; griddle is easiest. See <a href="/grilled-shrimp-compact-grill.html">shrimp</a> and <a href="/grilled-fish-compact-grill.html">fish</a>.</td></tr><tr><td>Vegetables</td><td>Cut flat surfaces, oil, high heat. See <a href="/grilled-vegetables-compact-grill.html">veggies</a>.</td></tr></table></div>
<h2>Troubleshooting</h2><div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Cause</th><th>Fix</th></tr><tr><td>Gray, no crust</td><td>Wet surface or weak heat</td><td>Dry food, more coals, wait for ashed coals</td></tr><tr><td>Sticks and tears</td><td>Flipped too early, grate cold or dirty</td><td>Preheat, scrape, oil; wait for release</td></tr><tr><td>Black outside, raw inside</td><td>Too long on high heat for the thickness</td><td>Sear then move to cool side</td></tr><tr><td>Bitter, sooty taste</td><td>Flare-ups from dripping fat</td><td>Trim fat, move food to cool side during flames</td></tr><tr><td>Uneven marks</td><td>Coals uneven under the grate</td><td>Spread hot coals evenly under the sear zone</td></tr></table></div>
{TEMPS}
""","fire",[("How do I get a good sear on a small charcoal grill?","Dry the food, oil it lightly, pile coals deep under one side, preheat the grates, and leave the food alone until it releases. Finish thick cuts on the cool side."),("Is lump charcoal better for searing?","Lump generally lights faster and burns hotter, which many cooks prefer for searing. Briquettes also work with a fuller chimney."),("Can I use a cast-iron skillet on the Slot Grill Compact?","Yes. Slot Grill describes the Compact as strong enough to support cast iron. A Slot Grill griddle (plancha) is another full-contact option."),("When should I flip a steak?","When the crust has formed and the steak releases from the grate on its own. For thick steaks, flipping more often after the first crust gives more even cooking.")])

page("cooking-for-a-crowd-small-grill","Cooking for a Crowd on a Small Charcoal Grill","How to feed a group from a 146 sq in Slot Grill Compact: menu planning, cooking in waves, holding food safely, refueling, and running two grills.",IMG["both16"],f"""
<p>The Slot Grill Compact is sized for one to three people. But owners take it to group camping trips, tailgates and job sites all the time, and it can feed more people than you'd think if you plan for it. The secret is to stop thinking about one big batch and start thinking in <b>waves</b>.</p><blockquote>"fantastic grill!!! Went with a group of people and everybody loved using it!!!"<cite>Cheryl Norris, Compact owner</cite></blockquote>
<h2>The math of a small grate</h2><p>The cooking area is 16.25" × 9" (146 sq in). How many pieces fit depends on size and how much space you leave to move things, so count at home: lay your burgers, sausages or chicken on a sheet pan in a 16" × 9" rectangle. That's one wave. Then plan how many waves you need and how long each takes.</p>
<h2>Pick a crowd-friendly menu</h2><ul><li><b>Foods that hold well</b> after cooking: sausages, brats, chicken thighs, <a href="/chicken-wings-small-charcoal-grill.html">wings</a>, pulled or sliced meats, <a href="/sausage-and-peppers-compact-grill.html">sausage and peppers</a>.</li><li><b>Foods that cook fast</b>: <a href="/smash-burgers-on-a-griddle.html">smash burgers</a>, <a href="/grilled-shrimp-compact-grill.html">shrimp</a>, skewers, thin steak.</li><li><b>Foods that are better shared</b>: <a href="/steak-fajitas-compact-grill.html">fajitas</a> and tacos stretch one steak across many people.</li><li><b>Sides that don't need the grill</b>: slaw, beans, chips, salads, fruit. Save grill space for protein.</li><li><b>Avoid</b> anything that needs a long, slow cook and a lot of space unless you have time.</li></ul>
<h2>Plan the waves</h2><ol><li><b>Wave 1, holders:</b> sausages or chicken. They're forgiving and hold in a covered pan.</li><li><b>Wave 2, the main event:</b> burgers or steak while people are ready to eat.</li><li><b>Wave 3, fast extras:</b> peppers and onions on the griddle, shrimp, vegetables.</li><li><b>Wave 4, dessert:</b> <a href="/smores-and-grilled-desserts-compact-grill.html">s'mores and grilled fruit</a> on the dying coals.</li></ol>
<h2>Keep the fire going</h2><ul><li>Start with a full chimney.</li><li>Light a second chimney about 20–30 minutes in, on a safe non-combustible surface, so you have lit coals ready to add.</li><li>Keep the <a href="/two-zone-fire-on-a-small-grill.html">two-zone</a> layout: the cool end is your holding shelf.</li><li>Push coals together and add fuel to the hot side; see <a href="/charcoal-temperature-control-small-grill.html">temperature control</a>.</li></ul>
<h2>Holding food safely</h2><p>Cooked food should stay hot (140°F or above) or be cooled and refrigerated. Food-safety guidance is not to leave perishable food out more than 2 hours, or 1 hour when it's above 90°F outside.</p><ul><li>Move finished food to a foil pan, cover with foil, and set it at the cool end of the grill or in an insulated cooler (no ice) lined with towels.</li><li>Use separate platters and tongs for raw and cooked food.</li><li>Keep raw meat in the cooler until its wave.</li></ul>
<h2>Use the griddle to double capacity</h2><p>A <a href="{GRID}">Slot Grill griddle (plancha)</a> turns part of the top into a flat surface that holds chopped or small food that would fall through a grate: onions, peppers, shrimp, sliced steak, eggs. It also keeps cooked items warm when set over the cool side.</p>
<h2>Running two grills</h2><p>For bigger groups, two Slot Grills side by side give you a dedicated sear grill and a dedicated hold-and-griddle grill. Owners who cook for families and groups often pair the Compact with the <a href="/compact-vs-full-size-slot-grill.html">Full Size</a>, which has a 20.5" × 11.25" (231 sq in) cooking area. Both flat-pack.</p>
<h2>Crowd packing list</h2><ul><li>Extra charcoal (more than you think) and a second chimney</li><li>Two pairs of tongs (raw and cooked), a spatula, heat-safe gloves</li><li>Instant-read thermometer</li><li>Foil pans and foil; insulated cooler for hot holding</li><li>Separate cooler with ice for raw meat</li><li>Paper towels, trash bags, hand-washing water and soap</li><li>Metal ash can with lid</li></ul>
<h2>Sample timeline for a group</h2><div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>Time</th><th>Task</th></tr><tr><td>T−30 min</td><td>Set up grill, light first chimney, prep platters</td></tr><tr><td>T−10</td><td>Pour coals, build zones, preheat grates</td></tr><tr><td>T0</td><td>Wave 1: sausages/chicken; light second chimney about 20 minutes in</td></tr><tr><td>T+30</td><td>Wave 2: burgers or steak; add lit coals to hot side</td></tr><tr><td>T+50</td><td>Wave 3: griddle veggies, shrimp</td></tr><tr><td>T+70</td><td>Dessert on dying coals; let fire burn down</td></tr></table></div>
{TEMPS}
""","fire",[("How many people can the Slot Grill Compact feed?","It's sized for one to three people per batch, but by cooking in waves and holding food safely you can feed a larger group. For big groups, a second Slot Grill helps."),("How do I keep food warm while I cook the next batch?","Cover finished food in a foil pan at the cool end of the grill or in an insulated cooler with no ice. Keep hot food at 140°F or above."),("How do I keep the fire going for a long group cook?","Start with a full chimney, light a second chimney partway through, and add lit coals to the hot side as the first batch fades."),("What's the best food to grill for a crowd on a small grill?","Foods that hold well (sausages, chicken thighs, wings) and foods that cook fast or stretch far (smash burgers, shrimp, fajitas).")])
